# Simultaneous Bottom‐Up Interfacial and Bulk Defect Passivation in Highly Efficient Planar Perovskite Solar Cells using Nonconjugated Small‐Molecule Electrolytes

## abstract
Recent perovskite solar cell (PSC) advances have pursued strategies for reducing interfacial energetic mismatches to mitigate energy losses, as well as to minimize interfacial and bulk defects and ion vacancies to maximize charge transfer. Here nonconjugated multi-zwitterionic small-molecule electrolytes (NSEs) are introduced, which act not only as charge-extracting layers for barrier-free charge collection at planar triple cation PSC cathodes but also passivate charged defects at the perovskite bulk/interface via a spontaneous bottom-up passivation effect. Implementing these synergistic properties affords NSE-based planar PSCs that deliver a remarkable power conversion efficiency of 21.18% with a maximum VOC = 1.19 V, in combination with suppressed hysteresis and enhanced environmental, thermal, and light-soaking stability. Thus, this work demonstrates that the bottom-up, simultaneous interfacial and bulk trap passivation using NSE modifiers is a promising strategy to overcome outstanding issues impeding further PSC advances.

Recent perovskite solar cell (PSC) advances have pursued strategies for reducing interfacial energetic mismatches to mitigate energy losses, as well as to minimize interfacial and bulk defects and ion vacancies to maximize charge transfer. Here nonconjugated multi-zwitterionic small-molecule electrolytes (NSEs) are introduced, which act not only as charge-extracting layers for barrier-free charge collection at planar triple cation PSC cathodes but also passivate charged defects at the perovskite bulk/interface via a spontaneous bottom-up passivation effect. Implementing these synergistic properties affords NSE-based planar PSCs that deliver a remarkable power conversion efficiency of $21.78\%$ with a maximum $V_{\mathrm{OC}}=1.19\:\mathrm{V},$ in combination with suppressed hysteresis and enhanced environmental, thermal, and light-soaking stability. Thus, this work demonstrates that the bottom-up, simultaneous interfacial and bulk trap passivation using NSE modifiers is a promising strategy to overcome outstanding issues impeding further PSC advances.
Hybrid organic–inorganic perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted great scientific and technological interest due to unparalleled processing-performance photovoltaic characteristics.[1] Due to improvements in both perovskite active layer compositions and the use of newly developed interfacial materials, PSC power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) have increased steadily from $3.8\%$ to $23.4\%$ .[2] The electron/hole transporting interfacial layers fulfill crucial functions in optimizing energy level alignment, charge transfer, charge trap passivation, and the operational stability of PSCs.[3] At the cathode side, electron extraction is enhanced by using an electron transporting layer (ETL). For devices with an n–i–p architecture, the most common ETLs are metal oxides such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ , and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ due to their commercial availability and excellent opto-electronic properties,[4] with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ being the most investigated.[5] However, efficient PSCs require that the ETL and cathode work functions (WFs) be sufficiently balanced to transfer electrons from the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital of the perovskite composition so as to minimize the device energy loss.[6] Compared with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $(\approx4.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ , the higher WF $(\approx4.5\ \mathrm{eV})$ and enriched surface defects of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ lead to lower PCEs, as a result of eroded open-circuit voltages $(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\mathrm{^{-}s})$ and fill factors (FFs), as well as severe hysteresis compared with PSCs based on mesoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .[3a,7] These defects limit further enhancement of photovoltaic performance, thus, optimizing the interface between the metal oxide and the perovskite layer remains very challenging.
Several strategies have been explored to address the above issues. In one approach, organic materials such as self-assembled monolayers (SAMs)[8] and conjugated polyelectrolytes (CPEs)[9] have been used to modify the metal oxide surface and achieve lower WF values via a mechanism involving the formation of permanent dipole moments. Furthermore, this approach passivates surface defects, greatly enhancing PSC electron extraction and reducing energy loss. For example, Yang’s group used 4-pyridinecarboxylic acid SAMs to modify the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL and achieved an excellent PCE of ${\approx}19\%$ in a planar PSC structure with a $0.04\mathrm{~V~}$ increased $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ (from 1.06 to 1.10  V).[10] However, this SAM deposition process is not ideal for roll-to-roll fabrication.[11] For solution processed CPEs, Lee’s group introduced paired CPEs to increase the built-in potential, yielding a remarkable PCE of $19.45\%$ and a $0.02\mathrm{~V~}$ increased $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ (from 1.08 to $1.10\ \mathrm{eV}]$ .[12] Since CPE dipole formation is strongly sensitive to the positions of the mobile positive and negative ions, thus details of functionality, polarity, and molecular stacking, the ability of CPEs to alter WFs is limited.[13] Consequently, nonconjugated insulating polymers with aliphatic amine groups (i.e., polyethylenimine, ethoxylated-polyethylenimine)[14] and molecular zwitterions[15] are emerging as promising interfacial modifiers due to the structural simplicity, stability, and marked tendency to form net permanent dipole moments.[16] For example, Park et  al. used a zwitterion with a low solubility in polar solvents to passivate ETL surfaces to increase $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ from 1.10 to $1.16\mathrm{V}$ and achieved a PCE of $\approx21\%$ .[17] In contrast, Huang et al. developed a highly soluble zwitterion for doping the perovskite layer to passivate bulk defects, providing similar performance.[18] However, to the best of our knowledge, molecular ETLs containing multiple zwitterionic units and, more importantly, those exhibiting both dual WF adjustment and passivation roles have not been demonstrated.
Here, we introduce two nonconjugated small-molecule electrolytes (NSEs), $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(((methyl(4-sulphonatobtyl)amonio) bis(propane-3,1-diyl))bis(dimethyl-ammoniumdiyl))bis(butane-1-sulfonate) (MSAPBS) and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -((oxybis-(ethane-2,1- diyl))bis(dimethylammoniumdiyl))-bis(butane-1-sulfonate)
(OEABS) having two and three zwitterionic units, respectively (Figure 1a),[15d,19] as effective and scalable modifying layers for planar PSCs. These NSEs achieve an ideal WF for the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, thus enabling barrier-free electron transfer, as well as passivation of the interface defects between the perovskite and ETL, preventing back electron transfer and suppressing charge recombination. Furthermore, the present data indicate that both OEABS and MSAPBS molecules partially diffuse into the perovskite photoactive layer, passivating bulk defects and ion vacancies at the perovskite grain boundaries via a spontaneous bottom-up passivation process. The result is that, compared with planar PSCs fabricated with traditional $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETLs, which afford PCEs of ${\approx}19\%$ , the cells based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{NSE}$ ETLs deliver PCEs as high as $21.18\%$ with an ultrahigh $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.19{\mathrm{~V}}.$ Furthermore, PSCs based on NSEs exhibit strongly suppressed hysteresis as well as enhanced environmental and light soaking stabilities. These results demonstrate that the NSEs reported here are very effective modifiers for achieving high-performance PSCs through a synergistic, bottom-up passivation effect of energy level alignment and surface/ bulk defect passivation. Thus, this novel bottom-up passivation mechanism provides a new pathway to overcome defects in PSC devices not limited to NSEs but extendable to other materials.
PSC devices were fabricated with a planar $\mathrm{{\n-i-p}}$ type architecture of structure indium tin oxide $(\mathrm{ITO})/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $(10~\mathrm{nm})/$ NSE $(8-9~\mathrm{nm})$ /perovskite $(400\ \mathrm{nm})$ /Spiro-OMeTAD $(300~\mathrm{nm})/$ Au $(100\ \mathrm{nm})$ (Figure  1b) and fabrication details are reported in the Supporting Information. A triple cation halide perovskite $\left(\mathrm{Cs}_{0.05}\mathrm{FA}_{0.81}\mathrm{MA}_{0.14}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2.55}\mathrm{Br}_{0.45}\right)$ was used as the photoactive material.[20] The device cross-section scanning electron microscope (SEM) image is shown in Figure  1c. Importantly, the process for the modification of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer with the NSE and of the photoactive layer deposition was optimized and involves six major steps (i–vi, Figure  1d): i) A $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer is deposited on the ITO substrate by spin-coating a nanoparticle dispersion ( $2.67~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ in water), then annealing at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . ii) A dense NSE layer is deposited on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface by spin-coating an NSE solution $(2{\mathrm{~mg~mL^{-1}}}$ in $\mathrm{MeOH{:}A c O H}=0.97{:}0.03$ v:v), then annealing at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10~\mathrm{\min}$ . From preliminary experiments, the optimal thicknesses of the pristine MSAPBS and OEABS films are ${\approx}8{\mathrm{-}}9\ \mathrm{nm}$ , as determined by X-ray reflectivity (see Figure S1 and Table S1, Supporting Information). iii) The perovskite precursor solution (dimethyl sulfoxide:N,N-dimethylformamide (DMSO:DMF) $=4{:}1$ v:v, $20~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}})$ ) is then drop-cast onto the NSE substrate and then spun at $4000~\mathrm{rpm}$ . iv) During the perovskite precursor spinning process, the pre-formed NSE film partially dissolves and the molecules diffuse into the perovskite precursor film due to the upward centrifugal force. v) $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the antisolvent (chlorobenzene) is dispensed while the sample is spinning to freeze all constituents into a uniform intermediate phase. vi) The precursor is converted into the highly uniform and crystalline perovskite phase by annealing at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ .
The NSE film morphology on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ was first investigated by atomic force microscopy (AFM). Both the pristine and DMSO:DMF “washed” $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{NSE}$ films were investigated, with the latter used to simulate the action of the perovskite precursor solution on the underlying NSE film during device fabrication (see step iii above). The AFM images (Figure  2a and Figure S2, Supporting Information) indicate that the root mean square (RMS) surface roughness $(\sigma_{\mathrm{RMS}})$ of the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}$ film is considerably less $(0.19\ \mathrm{nm})$ than that of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer $(0.99\mathrm{nm})$ ), thus revealing an amorphous and smooth film morphology. In contrast, the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{OEABS}$ $\sigma_{\mathrm{RMS}}$ is much larger $(1.40\ \mathrm{nm})$ ), with evidence of cracks propagating throughout the interior. After washing with DMF:DMSO, the MSAPBS films remain smooth and continuous with only a marginal increase in the $\sigma_{\mathrm{RMS}}$ to $0.40\mathrm{nm}$ . In contrast, under the same conditions, the OEABS films become severely discontinuous and exhibit a large $\sigma_{\mathrm{RMS}}$ of $3.29\mathrm{nm}$ . Furthermore, both the AFM image and the cross-section height profile evidence that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ underlayer is visible. To verify the change in thickness and electron density of the NSE films after washing,
X-ray reflectivity (XRR) results are presented in Figure S1 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information. After washing, both MSAPBS and OEABS have similar film thicknesses but, especially for the OEABS, decreased electron density which indicates decreased amounts of the NSEs, in good agreement with the AFM measurements.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was next utilized to understand the compositional changes and bonding states of all the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{NSE}$ films (Figures S3 and S4, Supporting Information). From the XPS data, the elemental composition of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{NSE}$ films was calculated and the results are shown in Figure  2b,c. Due to the electron scattering, the XPS detection depth of the present films is around $5{-}10\ \mathrm{nm}$ , so that the beam passes through the NSE coating and reaches the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . Therefore, the $\mathrm{\DeltaS/Sn}$ atomic ratio can be used to quantify how the DMSO:DMF washing affects the material composition in the detection region. For the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}$ sample, the $\mathrm{\DeltaS/Sn}$ atomic ratio is large $_{(\approx2.5)}$ corroborating the ultrasmooth and pin-hole-free MSAPBS surface fully covering the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . The small amount of Sn detected may reflect the thickness variations of the MSAPBS film, approaching the detection limit. However, after DMF:DMSO washing, the $\mathrm{\DeltaS/Sn}$ atomic ratio decreases to ${\approx}1.3$ , in agreement with retention of a smooth film with a reduced electron density (vide supra). However, for the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{OEABS}$ film the $\mathrm{\DeltaS/Sn}$ atomic ratio is low to begin $(\approx1.2)$ and after solvent washing it decreases considerably to ${\approx}0.2$ , a result fully consistent with the OEABS film morphologies assessed by AFM and electron density from XRR. We speculate that the differences are due to the totally different morphologies of these two NSEs before washing as well as the different affinities of these electrolytes for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . To evaluate the influence of the NSEs on the electronic structure of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, the work functions (WFs) of all samples were measured by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS), and the results are shown in Figure 2d,e. The WF of the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ film determined is $4.54~\mathrm{eV},$ in agreement with the literature,[21] and decreases considerably to 3.84 and $3.36\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the OEABS and MSAPBS coated films, respectively. The WF reduction when using our NSEs is corroborated by Kelvin Probe (KP) measurements (Table S2, Supporting Information, $\mathrm{WF}_{\mathrm{SnO}_{2}}=4.94\ \mathrm{eV}\rightarrow\mathrm{WF}_{\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{OEABS}}=4.58\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathrm{WF}_{\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}}=7\ \mathrm{SnO}_{4}\cdot78\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathrm{WF}_{\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}}=7\ \mathrm{SnO}_{4}\cdot78\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathrm{SnO}_{4}=7.04\ \ \mathrm{eV}\ .$ $4.30\ \mathrm{eV})$ ). Although the absolute WF values differ since UPS and KP measurements were carried out in ultrahigh vacuum and ambient, respectively, the same trend holds. The ultralow WF values are doubtless of the result of dipole formation due to ETL coverage with the zwitterionic molecules.[22] Importantly, in order to better simulate the interlayer energetics in these devices, we also measured the WFs of the NSE-coated samples by UPS after washing with DMF:DMSO as done for the morphological analysis, and they are found to be 4.04 and $3.78\ \mathrm{eV}$ for the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{OEABS}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}$ films, respectively. The resulting approximate energy band diagram based on the UPS data is shown in Figure  2f indicating negligible mismatch between the $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.81}M A_{0.14}P b I_{2.55}B r_{0.45}}$ perovskite conduction band minimum $(3.87~\mathrm{eV})$ and the ETL, thus suggesting a negligible energy barrier for electron extraction.[23] In addition, vertical charge transport in the modified interlayers was assessed in diodes of structure $\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/N S E/A g}$ . Here, the NSE films are not washed to measure their intrinsic conductivity. Representative $I{-}V$ characteristics are shown in Figure S5 in the Supporting Information and the extracted conductivity values with the film thickness are shown in Table S3 in the Supporting Information.[24] The pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ films exhibit a conductivity of $7.63\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ while those of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{OEABS}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MS}$ APBS films are lower and similar, $(1.45\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}})$ and $(6.32\times10^{-5}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}})$ , respectively, and the latter should provide a better platform for efficient charge extraction.
Next, $400\mathrm{nm}$ perovskite films $(\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.81}M A_{0.14}P b I_{2.55}B r_{0.45}})$ were deposited on both the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and NSE-modified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ substrates and the PSC devices completed by deposition of the hole transport layer (HTL; Spiro-OMeTAD, $300\mathrm{nm}$ ) and Au $(100\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) contacts. Details are reported in the Supporting Information. Before device characterization, the perovskite film morphologies were investigated by SEM and grazing incident X-ray diffraction (GIXRD). The SEM data (Figure S6a–c, Supporting Information) indicate that all films have a typical perovskite crystalline morphology with compact and close-knit flat grains with very close average grain sizes of 264, 259, and $271~\mathrm{nm}$ on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , OEABS/ $\mathrm{\DeltaSnO}_{2}$ , and MSAPBS/ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ substrates, respectively. The corresponding GIXRD patterns are also very similar and evidence two main reflections at $2\Theta=14.4^{\circ}$ and $28.8^{\circ}$ corresponding to the perovskite (110) and (220) planes (Figure S6d, Supporting Information).[25] Note that, independent of the substrate, all of these perovskite films exhibit sharp and clean Bragg reflections, in agreement with the large grain size and crystal morphology imaged by SEM.
Remarkably, the NSEs appear not to affect the perovskite topsurface/bulk morphology and microstructure despite the great impact on device performance (vide infra). Thus, to provide evidence on how the present NSEs operate, thinner perovskite films (60, 100, and $150~\mathrm{nm}$ ) on top of both pristine and NSEmodified $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ were investigated by SEM and GIXRD, and the data were compared to those obtained for the thicker films. The SEM images (Figure  3a and Figure S7, Supporting Information) indicate that the thinner perovskite films on the bare $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL exhibit the formation of a large number of pinholes/ cracks, which are greatly suppressed in the NSE-based samples, demonstrating that the present interlayers can bridge/ heal grain boundaries in the perovskite layer in contact with the electrode, especially for MSAPBS, eventually providing a compact perovskite morphology near the perovskite bulk interface region.[18] Furthermore, the GIXRD data for the thinner perovskite layers (Figure  3a and Figure S8, Supporting Information) further support the NSE positive effects. Thus, the diffraction peaks at $2\Theta=11.9^{\circ}$ and $13.0^{\circ}$ corresponding to the photoinactive hexagonal $\delta$ phase of the perovskite and the cubic $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , respectively, are clearly visible in the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL.[26] However, upon NSE intercalation both peaks are strongly/completely suppressed, demonstrating the bottom-up passivation and coordination at grain boundaries due to NSE molecular diffusion into the lower portion of the perovskite layer.[4c]
Next, XPS depth profile (Figure S9, Supporting Information and Figure  3b) analysis of the different perovskite/ETLs provides further details on the defect passivation chemistry. The characteristic peak area variation of Pb 4f, Sn 3d, and In 3d as a function of the sputtering time is used to monitor the perovskite/ETL layer compositions. Most interestingly, for the perovskite layer, depth profile analysis evidences the presence of two chemical components in the Pb 4f spectrum. One is metallic lead $\mathrm{[Pb^{0}}$ , with $\mathrm{Pb}4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}$ component at ${\approx}137~\mathrm{eV}$ binding energy) which is ascribed to the highly unsaturated $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ with removal of halide ions $(\Gamma^{-},\ \mathrm{Br^{-}})$ .[27] The other peak is assigned to $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ in the perovskite structure $\mathrm{(Pb~4f_{7/2}}$ component at $\approx139\ \mathrm{eV})$ ).[28] The depth profile of the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ to ${\mathrm{Pb}}^{0}$ atomic ratio in Figure  3b indicates that the $\mathrm{Pb^{\circ}}$ content increases with the sputtering time (depth) for all samples, and thus the $\mathrm{Pb}$ ion vacancies and defect densities increase near/at the perovskite– ETL interface. Note, however, that the films grown on the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{NSE}$ ETLs suppress the $\mathrm{Pb^{\circ}}$ content not only at the interface but also in the bulk, demonstrating the dual effect of these NSE molecules.
To further confirm the nanostructures and the origin of the bottom-up NSE self-doping effect on the photoactive perovskite a)Average values and standard deviation of 20 devices. Data in the parenthesis are for the champion devices; b)Integrated value bulk (vide infra), high-magnification cross-section images together with the associated element mapping were obtained from the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}$ ETLs which afford the best PSC performance (vide infra). The high-magnification bright field scanning transmission electron microscope (BF-STEM) images of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}$ ETL (Figure S10, Supporting Information) indicate that the thicknesses of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and MSAPBS films are ${\approx}10$ and ${\approx}9\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , respectively, and the associated interface is relatively smooth. This result further corroborates the XRR, AFM, and XPS data indicating that although perovskite film deposition reduces the XRR-derived MSAPBS electron density from 0.56 to $0.49\mathrm{~e~}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-3}$ (vide supra XRR), it does not degrade the film morphology or integrity. Energy dispersive spectrum (EDS) mapping of the near-bottom region of the PSC (Figure 3d) reveals the presence of In, Sn, S, and $\mathrm{Pb}$ , in accord with ITO, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , MASPBS, and perovskite layers, respectively. Well-defined elemental regions are observed for In and Sn due to the structural integrity of ITO and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . As for S, there is clearly a more intense region near the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ interface, corresponding to the MASPBS film and demonstrating the layer uniformity and good adhesion to the bottom ETL. The inhomogenous $\mathrm{Pb}$ elemental regions of the perovskite layer may be caused by the uneven thickness of the TEM sample, which is prepared by focused ion beam. However, S is also diffused into the perovskite film, in agreement with partial dissolution of the MSAPBS molecules in the perovskite precursor solution.
From the combined morphological and elemental analysis results, it is reasonable to propose that the NSE molecules modify PSC operation via two independent, but synergistic, effects as shown schematically for MSAPBS in Figure 3c. One effect is to act as a $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ surface modifier by passivating surface traps and reducing the ETL WF. Another effect is neutralizing perovskite layer charged defects both at the bottom interface and in the grain boundaries and bulk, using charged molecular components as effective dopants.[29] In the zwitterionic NSE structure with ammonium and sulfonate groups, the ammonium effectively passivates the cationic vacancies in the halide lattice while the sulfonate group passivates coordinatively unsaturated Pb(II) ions and zero-valent Pb.[29,30] Due to the specific physicochemical characteristic of these molecules and the multi-zwitterionic structure tightly bounding the molecules to the perovskite crystal boundaries, this dual-function synergistic effect spontaneously occurs during PSC fabrication without the need of complex process control.
$J{-}V$ hysteresis tests were next carried out to quantify the PSC intrinsic stability (Figure  4d). Generally, PSC hysteresis is ascribed to interfacial capacitance which originates from ion migration, abundant bulk and surface traps, and unbalanced charge transport.[34] The hysteresis index, $(\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{Reverse}}\mathrm{~-~}\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{Forward}})/\mathrm{PCE}_{\mathrm{Reverse}}$ of the devices fabricated with $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{OEABS}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{MSAPBS/SnO_{2}}$ is $11.45\%$ $7.26\%$ , and $6.20\%$ , respectively. The data show that the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ NSE-based devices exhibit significantly suppressed hysteresis versus those based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ alone due to the well-matched charge balance and trap passivation achieved with the NSEs. Furthermore, we measured the photocurrent of the champion devices under an applied bias at the maximum power point (AM 1.5G illumination) to determine the real power output.
Thus, the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{OEABS}$ , and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}$ based devices exhibit stabilized efficiencies of $18.73\%$ , $19.34\%$ , and $20.38\%$ at the maximum power point (0.93, 0.95, and 0.99  V) values consistent with the PCEs measured from the $J{-}V$ sweeps (Figure 4a). Next, temporal stability measurements in the dark at different temperatures (Figure  4e,f) were conducted for unencapsulated devices and measured periodically under opencircuit conditions. All devices tested at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{RH}\approx25\%$ exhibit excellent air stability (PCE retention $590\%$ after $700\mathrm{~h~}$ ) due to the stable perovskite composition and the present interfacial materials. Furthermore, we evaluated these devices under more severe conditions at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathrm{RH}\approx85\%$ according to an international standard (IEC 61215, Figure S14, Supporting Information). While the neat $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ devices degrade completely within $160\mathrm{~h~}$ , those based on OEABS and MSAPBS are far more stable, retaining $\approx60\%$ and $75\%$ , respectively, of the initial PCE after $160\mathrm{~h~}$ operation. Finally, light-soaking stability measurements $(100\mathrm{\mw\cm^{-2}}$ white light-emitting diode (LED), $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ lowing box, $T=55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , see Figure S15, Supporting Information) were also carried out and the data presented in Figure $4\mathrm{g}$ . After ${\approx}200\mathrm{~h~}$ light soaking, the PSCs based on both MSAPBS and OEABS retain a higher PCE ( $66\%$ and $60\%$ , respectively) compared with those based on the neat $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ $(49\%)$ . Details of the device performance metrics during the stability tests are shown in Figure S16 and Table S5 in the Supporting Information. Overall the combined measurements support a greatly improved stability when using the NSEs because of efficient defect passivation.[35]
Finally, to understand the sizable PSC $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ increases with the NSEs, Mott–Schottky curves from the capacitance–voltage $(C-V)$ data are presented in Figure S21 in the Supporting Information, and the $x$ -intercept of a straight-line in the linear part of the plot provides the flat-band potential $(V_{\mathrm{FB}})$ values. Because the Spiro-OMeTAD was used as the HTL in all devices, the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ is directly related to $V_{\mathrm{FB}}$ . The $V_{\mathrm{FB}}$ value of the devices based on $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{OEABS}$ (0.94 V) and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{MSAPBS}$ (0.97 V) are considerably higher than that of the pristine $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (0.85  V) owing to the interfacial dipoles of the thin NSE layers. These results are in good agreement with the WF data and the measured $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values of these devices.[39]
In summary, we demonstrate the use of NSE molecules as dual-functional modifiers to enhance the performance and stability of PSCs. One clear effect of functionalizing the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL with an NSE is to strongly reduce the WF and passivate the ETL surface, yielding barrier-free charge transport. Another effect is to neutralize charged defects at the bottom interface and grain boundaries of the perovskite film. As a result, compared with PSCs fabricated on the traditional $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ETL, which afford PCEs of ${\approx}19\%$ , the present OEABS and MSAPBS based devices deliver remarkable PCEs of $19.82\%$ ( $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /OEABS) and $21.18\%$ $\mathrm{(SnO_{2}/M S A P B S)}$ ), with the latter having an ultrahigh $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of 1.19 V. Furthermore, PSCs based on the present NSEs have significantly suppressed hysteresis and enhanced environmental stability. These results demonstrate that multi-zwitterionic NSEs are viable modifiers for PSCs interfaces, providing unique and synergistic performance enhancing electronic and structural effects.
